#uuid "e9d8d958-ca6a-534b-a36e-14f918f29429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311506 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f46fb3e1-c358-54d4-ba7b-332d199867ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311536 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcad9de3-0595-5b13-b359-6cabf54fe1a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "565a9d25-9f82-568f-a358-819477f18aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311847 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43a060a8-89ee-5d43-a547-481f4dd7a9ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311854 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14e95436-1992-5d26-a51a-862b1ae1c205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{6x})}^{′}-6(u{e}^{6x}) & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+6u{e}^{6x}-6u{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397061 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397062 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "abaac5ec-4860-5fab-abd5-9e1c5fafe889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397063 #edb/ref 1080 "x{e}^{-7x}" #